WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.879 --> 00:00:07.520
Every year, more than 100 countries send 6
of their brightest teenagers, or the occasional

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.340
pre-pubescent prodigy, to represent them in
the International Math Olympiad, commonly

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.389
known as the IMO.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.550
Considering that each country has its own
elaborate system of contests leading to their

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.390
choice of 6 representatives, the IMO stands
as the culminating symbol for the surprisingly

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.900
expansive and wonderful world which is contest
math.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:32.189
The contest is essentially a test, split over
two days, with three questions given over

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.510
four and a half hours each day.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:39.190
The questions are all proofs, meaning you
don’t simply find some numerical answer,

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.960
you have to discover and articulate a rigorous
line of reasoning to answer each difficult

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.920
question, and they are scored on a scale from
0 to 7.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:54.650
Of interest to us today is the one from 2011,
with 563 total participants representing 101

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:55.650
countries.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:59.600
I know what your thinking, and the answer
is yes.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.280
Those do all happen to be prime numbers.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.810
But that’s not why this test was interesting,
out of these prime problem solvers, only one,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.810
Lisa Saurmann from Germany, got a perfect
score.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:14.200
The only thing standing between the next two
runners up and a perfect score that year was

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.119
problem #2.

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:20.740
This problem is beautiful, and despite evading
many of the world’s best mathematicians

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:26.350
of their age, the solution is something which
anyone watching this video can understand.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.110
So let’s begin by reading it carefully.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:33.179
“Let S be a finite set of at least two points
in the plane”

00:01:33.179 --> 00:01:37.179
Mmkay, as you read a question it’s often
helpful to start drawing out an example for

00:01:37.179 --> 00:01:38.179
yourself.

00:01:38.179 --> 00:01:41.610
“Assume that no three points of S are collinear”

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:44.020
In other words, you never have three points
that line up.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:48.509
So you can probably predict that the problem
involves drawing lines here in a way where

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:51.039
three points on one line would mess things
up.

00:01:51.039 --> 00:01:56.819
“A windmill is a process that starts with
a line l going through a single point P in

00:01:56.819 --> 00:02:02.549
S. The line rotates clockwise about the pivot
P until the first time that the line meets

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:05.259
some other point belonging to S”.

00:02:05.259 --> 00:02:10.400
Again, while reading it’s helpful to draw
out an example, so we’ve got this line pivoting

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.750
around some point until it hits another.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:19.160
“This point, Q, takes over as the new pivot,
and the line now rotates clockwise about Q,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:23.060
until it next meets a point of S. This process
continues indefinitely”.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.960
Alright, that’s kind of fun.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.420
We keep rotating and changing the pivot.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.500
You can see why they call it a windmill process,
and you can also see why they specified that

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.920
no three points lie on one line.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.500
You wouldn’t want to run into the ambiguity
where you don’t know what pivot to switch

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.500
to.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.900
Okay, so what’s the question?

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:48.140
“Show that we can choose a point P in S
and a line l going through P such that the

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:55.560
resulting windmill uses each point of S as
a pivot infinitely many times.”

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.990
Depending on your tolerance of puzzles for
puzzles’ sake, you might wonder why anyone

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:01.540
would care about such a question.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.870
There’s a very good reason: I’d argue
that the act of solving this will make you

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:10.780
better at math and other related fields, which
I’ll explain once you’ve seen the solution.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.880
But certainly on it surface this feels disconnected
from other parts of math.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:21.120
Other Olympiad problems involve some function
to analyze, or a numerical pattern to deduce,

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.640
or perhaps a difficult counting setup or an
elaborate geometric construction, but problem

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.800
2 is an unusually pure puzzle.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.320
In some ways, that’s its charm: proving
that some initial condition will result in

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:38.750
this windmill hitting all points infinitely
many times doesn’t test if you know a particular

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.030
theorem, it tests if you can find a clever
perspective.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:48.380
But that blade cuts both ways; without resting
existing results from math, what could someone

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:49.850
possibly study that would prepare them to
solve this?

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:56.510
In fact, this brings us to the second unusual
thing about this problem: Based on the results,

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:00.560
I’m guessing it turned out to be much harder
than the contest organizers expected.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.250
You see, typically, the three problems in
each day are supposed to get progressively

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:06.250
harder.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:11.950
They’re all hard, of course, but problems
1 and 4 should be doable, problems 2 and 5

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:16.750
should be challenging, and problems 3 and
6...well they can be brutal.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:25.170
But take a look at how many of the 563 participants
that year got perfect scores on each problem:

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.620
Only 22 got a perfect score for this question
number 2.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:34.560
By contrast, 170 got a perfect score on problem
5, which is supposed to be about the same

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.230
difficulty, and more than twice as many, got
a perfect score for problem 3, which is supposed

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:41.040
to be harder.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.260
You might notice that only six students got
full points for problem 6 that year, so by

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.980
some measure that was the hardest problem
on the test.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:52.710
In fact, the way I introduced things earlier
was a little disingenuous, the full data would

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.520
suggest problem 6 was the real clincher.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.220
But what’s strange is if you look at the
results of the only six students to solve

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:05.620
problem 6, all of whom are clearly phenomenal,
world-class problem solvers, this windmill

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:09.940
puzzle evaded five out of six of them.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:14.041
But again, this problem is hard not hard because
of the background knowledge it demands, it

00:05:14.041 --> 00:05:16.360
asks only for insight.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:19.050
So how do you approach something like this?

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:23.190
The first step with any puzzle like this is
simply to play around to get a feel for it,

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:27.580
and it’s always good to start simple and
slowly get more complicated from there.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:33.450
The simplest case would be two points, where
the line trades off between each point, so

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:35.010
that works well enough.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:39.880
Adding a third point, it’s pretty clear
the line will just rotate around all of them.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:44.560
It might not be entirely clear how you’d
phrase that as a rigorous proof yet, but right

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.010
now you’re just getting a feel for things.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.260
The fourth point is where things get interesting.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:53.330
In some places, your windmill will go around
the four points as it did with the triangle,

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:59.380
but if we put it inside that triangle, then
it looks like our windmill never hits it.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:03.050
Looking back at the problem, it’s asking
you to show that for some starting position

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:10.199
of the line, not any position, the process
will hit all the points infinitely many times.

00:06:10.199 --> 00:06:13.830
So for an example like this, if you start
with the line going through that troublesome

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.860
middle point, what happens?

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.590
And again, at this point you're just playing
around, perhaps moving your pencil among dots

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:25.870
you’ve drawn on your scratch paper, questions
of rigor and proof will come later.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:29.940
Here you’d see that your windmill does indeed
bounce off all the points as it goes through

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.120
a cycle, and it ends up back where it started.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:38.030
The worry you might have is that in some large
set of points, where some are kind of inside

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:43.760
the others, you might be able to start off
on the inside, but maybe the windmill process

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:51.680
takes the line to the outside, where it will
be blocked off from those inner points.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.070
If you play around, and it might take some
time to draw out many examples and think this

00:06:56.070 --> 00:07:00.290
through, you might notice that when the line
starts off passing the middle of all your

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:08.240
points, it tends to stay there; it never seems
to venture off to the outside.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.949
But can you guarantee that this always happens?

00:07:12.949 --> 00:07:18.199
Or rather, can you first make this idea of
starting off in the middle more rigorous,

00:07:18.199 --> 00:07:39.990
and from there, prove that all points will
be hit infinitely many times?

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:45.360
As a general problem-solving tip, whenever
you have a vague idea that feels productive,

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:51.050
find a way to be more exact about what you
mean, preferably putting numbers to it, then

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.360
ask questions about those numbers.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.280
In our example, one way to formalize the idea
of a “middle” is to count how many points

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:00.490
are on either side of the line.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:04.961
If you give the line some orientation, you
can reasonably talk about the left half, say

00:08:04.961 --> 00:08:09.400
coloring all points to the left of it blue,
and the right half, coloring all points to

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:13.530
the right of it brown, and what it means for
the line to be in the middle is that there

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.840
are as many blue points and brown points.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:22.710
For the moment, let’s say the total number
of points is odd, and the point the line passes

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.350
through is colored white.

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:30.300
So for example, if there were 11 points, then
you’d have five blue ones on the left of

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.830
the line, and five brown ones on the right
of the line, and the one white one at the

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:34.830
pivot.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:39.400
The case with an even number of points will
be similar, just slightly less symmetric.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:44.210
This gives you a new question to ask: What
happens to the number of blue points and brown

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:49.120
points as the process plays out?

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:54.610
In the example shown, you might notice that
it’s always 5 and 5, never changing.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:58.440
Playing with other examples, you’d find
the same is true.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:12.340
Take a moment to pause and see if you can
think about why that would be true.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:24.690
Well, the key is to think through what happens
as a line changes its pivot.

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:28.710
Having given the line an orientation, we can
talk about which half of the line is “above”

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.430
the pivot, and which is “below”.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:37.380
If the line hits a blue point to its left,
it must happen below the pivot, so as it changes

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:42.850
pivots and continues rotating clockwise, the
old pivot, now above the new one, ends up

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:46.300
to the left of the line, in the blue region.

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:51.200
And entirely symmetrically, when it hits a
brown point, it happens above the pivot, meaning

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.110
that old pivot ends up in the brown region.

00:09:54.110 --> 00:10:02.920
So no matter what, the number of points on
a given side of the line can’t change.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:06.311
When you lose a blue point, you gain a new
one; when you lose a brown point, you also

00:10:06.311 --> 00:10:07.570
gain a new one.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:10.320
Great, that’s insight #1.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:15.560
Now why would this imply that the line must
hit every point infinitely many times, no

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:19.040
matter what weird set of points you dream
up?

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:25.990
Well, think about letting this process go
until the line has turned 180 degrees.

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:30.520
That means it’s parallel to its starting
position, and because it has to remain the

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:35.250
case that half the points are on one side,
half are on the other, it must be passing

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:37.830
through the same point it started on.

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:45.910
Otherwise, if it ended up on some other point,
it would change the number on a given side.

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:53.790
Additionally, since the line has rotated halfway
around, everything that was blue has become

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:59.250
brown, and everything which was brown has
become blue, and the only way to change color

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:07.540
is if you get hit by the line.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:12.901
So for our odd number case, that means that
after a half rotation, the line is back to

00:11:12.901 --> 00:11:16.339
where it started having hit all the other
points.

00:11:16.339 --> 00:11:21.230
So as time moves forward, it repeats that
exact same set of motions over and over, hitting

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:24.720
all points infinitely many times.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:33.000
If there are an even number of points, we
need to alter the scheme slightly, but only

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.000
slightly.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.930
Let’s say that in that case, the pivot counts
as a brown point.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:47.230
So we can still select an initial point so
that half the points are blue, all on the

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:53.080
left, and half are brown, now either meaning
their either on the right or the pivot.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:12:00.410
The same argument applies that after a 180-degree
turn, all the points will have swapped color,

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:04.320
although this time the line might be passing
through a different point after that first

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:08.279
half turn, specifically one that used to be
blue.

00:12:08.279 --> 00:12:11.750
But after another 180 degrees the line must
be passing through the same point it started

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:12.750
on.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:13.750
Why?

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:18.420
Again, it must be parallel to its starting
position, and if it was passing through any

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:23.100
other point, the number of points on a given
side would be different.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:27.959
So again, you have a cycle which hits all
points, and which ends in the same position

00:12:27.959 --> 00:12:37.360
where it started, so it must hit all points
infinitely many times.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:45.730
There are two important lessons to take away
from this puzzle, the first social, the second

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:47.550
mathematical.

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:53.560
Once you know this solution and turn it about
in your head a couple times, it’s very easy

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:57.980
to fool yourself into thinking the problem
is easier than it is.

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:02.930
Of course the number of points on a given
side stays constant, right?

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:07.010
And of course when you start in the middle,
every point will switch sides after a half-turn,

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:08.560
right?

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:12.810
But the advantage of this problem coming from
the IMO is that we don’t have to rest on

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:17.610
subjective statements, we have the data to
show that this is a genuinely hard problem,

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:22.990
in that it evaded many students are demonstrably
able to solve hard problems.

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:28.120
In math, it’s extremely hard to empathize
with what it feels like not to understand

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:29.120
something.

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:32.830
I was discussing this video with a former
coworker of mine from Khan Academy who worked

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:38.490
a lot on their math exercises, and he pointed
out that across the wide variety of contributors

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:39.930
he’d worked with, there was one constant.

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:44.110
No one can tell how difficult their exercises
are.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:48.089
Knowing when math is hard is often way harder
than the math itself.

00:13:48.089 --> 00:13:52.240
This is important to keep in mind when teaching,
but it’s equally important to keep in mind

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:53.709
when being taught.

00:13:53.709 --> 00:13:59.610
On are windmill puzzle, even if counting the
number of points on one side seems obvious

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:05.661
in hindsight, you have to ask, given the vast
space of possible things to consider, why

00:14:05.661 --> 00:14:10.050
would anyone’s mind ever turn to this particular
idea?

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:12.500
This brings us to the mathematical takeaway.

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:17.140
What ultimately led to the solution was finding
something about this complex system which

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:20.930
stays constant during its chaotic unfolding.

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:23.930
This is a ubiquitous theme through math and
physics.

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:27.160
We’re finding what’s called an “invariant”.

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:32.260
Topologists do this when the count the holes
in a surface, physicists do this in defining

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:38.970
the ideas of energy and momentum, or in special
relativity when they define proper time.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:43.050
As a student, it’s easy to take for granted
the definitions handed down to us, but the

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:48.279
more puzzles you solve where the insight involves
an invariant, the more you appreciate that

00:14:48.279 --> 00:14:53.870
each of these definitions was once a clever
discovery.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:58.660
Terence Tao, one of the greatest modern mathematicians
and the world’s youngest IMO medalist, wrote

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:03.209
that “Mathematical problems, or puzzles,
are important to real mathematics (like solving

00:15:03.209 --> 00:15:07.870
real-life problems), just as fables, stories,
and anecdotes are important to the young in

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:09.970
understanding real life.”

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:14.980
Sure, they’re contrived, but they carry
lessons relevant to useful problems you may

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.600
actually need to solve one day.

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:22.110
Maybe it seems silly to liken this windmill
puzzle to a fairytale; a mathematical Aesop

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:26.860
summarizing that the moral of the story is
to seek quantities which stay constant.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:30.959
But some of you watching this will one day
face a problem where finding an invariant

00:15:30.959 --> 00:15:35.209
reveals a slick solution, and you might even
look like a genius for doing so.

00:15:35.209 --> 00:15:39.790
If a made-up windmill prepares you for a real
problem, who cares that it’s a fiction?

